  <h2>When you need a written equipment rental agreement</h2>
  <p>
    Rental houses always have one — theirs. It is usually printed on the back of the delivery ticket
    and written entirely for the lessor. When you rent from another contractor, or rent your own
    machine out, there is often nothing in writing at all until a boom lift comes back with a
    cracked platform. This template gives both sides the same page to agree on: what was rented, at
    what rate, for how many hours, who operates it, who pays when it is damaged, and what condition
    it must come back in.
  </p>

  <h2>Bare rental vs. operated rental</h2>
  <p>
    The most important box on each schedule row is <strong>bare</strong> or <strong>operated</strong
    >. On a bare rental the renter supplies the operator and takes responsibility for operation,
    rigging, loads and ground conditions, and must use operators who are trained, qualified and
    certified as the law and the manufacturer require. On an operated rental the lessor supplies the
    operator, who stays the lessor’s employee; the renter still directs the work and is responsible
    for site access, ground bearing and the loads and signals it provides. Section 3 of the terms
    spells out that split, and the operator can refuse any lift they consider unsafe.
  </p>

  <h2>Rates, hours and the fine print that costs money</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Rate basis.</strong> Hour, day, week or month per item. A partial period is charged at the
      lower of the next shorter rate or the full period.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hours included.</strong> A day, week or month includes a set number of engine hours; extra
      hours are charged pro rata from the hour meter readings on the condition record.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Off rent.</strong> Rent runs until the lessor picks the machine up after your off-rent call.
      Get an off-rent number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fuel and cleaning.</strong> Return it full (or as delivered) and clean, or pay the refuel
      and cleaning charges.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Breakdowns.</strong> Stop using and report a broken machine; rent does not run while it
      is down for a breakdown you did not cause.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Damage, loss and insurance</h2>
  <p>
    From delivery until pickup, the renter carries the risk of loss, theft and damage other than
    ordinary wear and tear or the lessor’s own negligence, and pays for repair — or fair market
    value if the machine is lost — plus rent while it is out of service, up to a reasonable period.
    There are two ways to cover that: buy the lessor’s damage waiver, which limits accidental damage
    but usually excludes theft, misuse and unqualified operators, or decline it and carry your own
    coverage for rented equipment at full replacement value with the lessor as loss payee. Either
    way, the lessor will want certificates for general liability (naming it additional insured),
    auto liability if you haul the equipment, and workers’ compensation before the truck leaves the
    yard.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example is Summit Mechanical renting from a Denver rental house for the
    rooftop-unit set on the Harbor Point medical office, August 3–28, 2026: a 60 ft boom lift at
    $1,450 a week for four weeks ($5,800), a 26 ft scissor lift at $395 a week ($1,580), a 10,000 lb
    telehandler at $525 a day for three days ($1,575), and a 40-ton crane with a certified operator
    at $245 an hour for eight hours ($1,960). Estimated rent is $10,915.00, plus $1,050.00 for
    delivery and pickup, for an estimated $11,965.00 before tax. Summit declines the damage waiver
    because its own policy covers rented equipment at full replacement value, and the hour
    allowances are 8 per day, 40 per week and 160 per month.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No condition record at delivery, so pre-existing damage becomes yours.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to call the machine off rent, and paying for another week.</li>
    <li>Letting an uncertified operator run a bare-rented machine that requires certification.</li>
    <li>Declining the damage waiver without confirming your policy covers rented equipment.</li>
    <li>
      Leaving the equipment cost out of the bid — see the <a
        href="/templates/construction-estimate/">construction estimate template</a
      >.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Once the machine is on site, its rental is a direct cost of that job. Code the invoices to the
    project so the rental shows up where it belongs —
    <a href="/blog/job-costing-for-subcontractors/">job costing for subcontractors</a> explains how equipment
    fits next to labor and materials.
  </p>
